.TH snowcone 1 "27 October 2026" "snowcone" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
\fBsnowcone \fP- IRC client and solanum server notice console
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.fam C
\fBsnowcone\fP \fIMODE\fP [\fB--config\fP=PATH] [key.path=value \.\.\.]

.fam T
.fi
.fam T
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBsnowcone\fP is a terminal IRC client for network operators. Its hub mode
connects to any number of networks at once, chats on them, and turns
server notices into live views of recent connections, exits, bans and
per-server load. It runs on a scripting environment whose code and
configuration can be reloaded without dropping connections.
.SH MODES
.TP
.B
hub
Chat and server notices on many networks. This page is about
hub mode.
.TP
.B
program.lua
A Lua program, given by its path. It has no terminal until it
calls \fBsnowcone.start_ui\fP(), and \fBsnowcone\fP exits once the program
has no work left.
.SH GETTING STARTED
hub reads ~/.config/\fBsnowcone\fP/hub.toml. A minimal configuration names
one network with a short id, how to reach it and a nick:
.PP
.nf
.fam C
        [[networks]]
        id = 'libera'

        [networks.server]
        host = 'irc.libera.chat'
        port = 6697

        [[networks.layers]]
        type = 'tls'

        [networks.identity]
        nick = 'mynick'

.fam T
.fi
Then run \fBsnowcone\fP hub. Add a [[networks]] table for each network. SASL,
operator login, proxies, client certificates, features and the other
settings are described in docs/CONFIG.md in the \fBsnowcone\fP source.
.PP
Settings can be overridden for one run on the command line, for example
\fBsnowcone\fP hub ui.profile=chat.
.SH USING HUB
The bottom line is the status bar: the view's name, then every network
colored by state (green connected, yellow on the way, red idle), with
+ or ! for unseen chat or a mention. Click a network, press Meta-1 to
Meta-9, or use /net to choose the current network. Views and commands
act on the current network.
.PP
Commands start with / and are typed on the bottom line. A command that
acts on a network can name another one with @id first, for example
/quote @oftc WHOIS someone.
.PP
Many things on screen can be clicked: a network, a column title to
sort by it, a connection to stage a K-Line for it, a message to inspect
it in the detail pane, or a [+N more] button to expand a list.
.PP
The detail pane opens above the view to show one thing in full: a
message, a /whois or /whowas reply, or a channel's topic, modes and
members (/chaninfo). Clicking a buffer's name in the status bar opens a
channel's information or a nick's /whois. A status line that starts
with \[u25B8] opens what it summarizes. While the pane is open it takes
the scrolling keys; Esc or its [CLOSE] closes it, and [DISCARD] also
drops it from the status lines that would reopen it.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B
/
Type a command
.TP
.B
t
Talk in the shown chat buffer
.TP
.B
a
Send an action (/me) to the shown chat buffer
.TP
.B
T
Send a notice to the shown chat buffer
.TP
.B
n, p
Next and previous chat buffer, in the buffer view
.TP
.B
C-s
Filter the current view, or the detail pane when it shows a /quote
reply, a /whowas or a channel
.TP
.B
Esc
Close the detail pane; else leave input, clear filters and scrolling
.TP
.B
C-n, C-p
Next and previous view of the rotation
.TP
.B
F1..F12
A view of the rotation
.TP
.B
Meta-1..9
Choose a network
.TP
.B
Meta-a
The next buffer with unseen messages, mentions first
.TP
.B
PgUp, PgDn
Scroll by a page; Up and Down by a line (the detail pane while it is open)
.TP
.B
Tab
